### The cross-tenant existence oracle, CLOSED (#137). This case used to pin the defect; it now pins the fix.
###
### `work_orders.reference` is declared `unique` on a `tenancy: "scoped"` entity. The driver emitted that
### as a plain `HasIndex(field.Name).IsUnique()` with **no `tenant_id`**, so the constraint held across the
### whole instance rather than within a tenant — and tenant south's create of a value only tenant NORTH
### held was refused, while the same create of a free value succeeded. The two requests differ in exactly
### one thing, so any observable difference between the answers WAS that fact, disclosed: tenant south
### could learn whether north held a given `reference`, one request per candidate.
###
### **The root cause was the index, not the status code.** The neighbouring defect — `030-Problems/002`,
### a duplicate answered 500 rather than 409 — was a *different* problem that happened to share a trigger,
### and mapping the violation to a clean 409 did NOT close this one: 409-versus-201 is exactly the same
### signal to tenant south as 500-versus-201 was. The fix is a tenant-scoped unique index,
### `(tenant_id, reference)` on a scoped entity, emitted after the field loop because
### `DescriptorToSchemaMapper` appends `tenant_id` last and EF refuses to resolve it mid-loop.
###
### So BOTH probes below are now **201**, and the test asserts they are **indistinguishable** — the
### assertion this case was written to be rewritten as. Uniqueness still holds inside a tenant: that is
### `030-Problems/002`, from tenant north, over a value north itself already holds.

### Tenant south creates a work order whose `reference` TENANT NORTH also holds. Accepted: the constraint
### is scoped to the tenant, so north's data does not answer south's request.
# @name ProbeAValueAnotherTenantHolds
## TEST-EXPECT-STATUS: [201]
POST {{fsBaseUrl}}/api/work_orders
X-Alvo-Api-Key: {{keyDispatcherSouth}}
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "tenant_id": "{{tenantSouth}}",
  "reference": "WO-1001",
  "title": "Oracle probe",
  "status": "scheduled",
  "priority": 1,
  "access_code": "ORACLE",
  "customer_id": "{{SouthCustomerId}}",
  "region_id": "{{RegionNorthId}}"
}

### The identical request with a `reference` NO tenant holds. The only difference between the two is
### whether the other tenant happens to hold the value — which is precisely the fact south must not learn,
### and the two answers now say nothing about it.
# @name ProbeAValueNoTenantHolds
## TEST-EXPECT-STATUS: [201]
POST {{fsBaseUrl}}/api/work_orders
X-Alvo-Api-Key: {{keyDispatcherSouth}}
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "tenant_id": "{{tenantSouth}}",
  "reference": "WO-9911",
  "title": "Oracle probe",
  "status": "scheduled",
  "priority": 1,
  "access_code": "ORACLE",
  "customer_id": "{{SouthCustomerId}}",
  "region_id": "{{RegionNorthId}}"
}

### Tenant south now holds its OWN WO-1001, and reading it back proves the row is south's rather than a
### view of north's: one reference, two rows, one per tenant.
# @name SouthAfterTheProbe
## TEST-EXPECT-STATUS: [200]
GET {{fsBaseUrl}}/api/work_orders?limit=100&order=reference.asc
X-Alvo-Api-Key: {{keyDispatcherSouth}}

### ...and tenant north's own WO-1001 is untouched by either probe: south's create added a row, it did not
### overwrite north's.
# @name NorthsRowAfterTheProbe
## TEST-EXPECT-STATUS: [200]
GET {{fsBaseUrl}}/api/work_orders/{{Wo1001Id}}
X-Alvo-Api-Key: {{keyDispatcherNorth}}

### Uniqueness still holds INSIDE a tenant: south repeating its own new reference is refused, and refused
### as a 409 naming the field rather than as a 500. Without this the fix would be indistinguishable from
### having dropped the constraint altogether.
# @name SouthRepeatsItsOwnReference
## TEST-EXPECT-STATUS: [409]
POST {{fsBaseUrl}}/api/work_orders
X-Alvo-Api-Key: {{keyDispatcherSouth}}
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "tenant_id": "{{tenantSouth}}",
  "reference": "WO-9911",
  "title": "Oracle probe",
  "status": "scheduled",
  "priority": 1,
  "access_code": "ORACLE",
  "customer_id": "{{SouthCustomerId}}",
  "region_id": "{{RegionNorthId}}"
}

### Clean up both rows the probes created, so later groups measure the set they expect. Both, now: the
### first probe used to be refused and left nothing behind.
# @name RemoveTheFirstProbeRow
## TEST-EXPECT-STATUS: [204]
DELETE {{fsBaseUrl}}/api/work_orders/{{ProbeAValueAnotherTenantHolds.response.body.$.id}}
X-Alvo-Api-Key: {{keyDispatcherSouth}}

###
# @name RemoveTheSecondProbeRow
## TEST-EXPECT-STATUS: [204]
DELETE {{fsBaseUrl}}/api/work_orders/{{ProbeAValueNoTenantHolds.response.body.$.id}}
X-Alvo-Api-Key: {{keyDispatcherSouth}}
